> In this methodology section, a two-stage instrumental variable approach is outlined to study the impact of agricultural productivity on real wages, focusing on the effect of employer-provided meals. It addresses the endogeneity issue of rice yields by using climate-based instruments, including Growing Degree Days and precipitation, which affect crop growth but not local wage-setting. The first stage models yields using these variables, while the second stage uses predicted yields to examine effects on real agricultural wages, accounting for variables like gender and meal provision. Fixed effects for district and season are included to manage unobserved heterogeneity.
