> This section introduces a theoretical framework to analyze wage contracts in informal agricultural labor markets, where compensation often includes both cash and in-kind elements like meals. This framework integrates two established theories: the efficiency wage theory, particularly its nutritional variant, and the compensating differential theory. It posits that meals can offset cash wages and enhance worker productivity through improved physical capabilities. Labor contracts are modeled as comprising cash wages and meals, with worker utility derived from the total compensation value, which includes the market value of meals and their additional non-monetary benefits.
